{"answer": "In a binary tree, **null pointers** waste memory because most nodes are **leaf nodes** (nodes with no children). [p.1] Since a leaf node has no left or right child, its child pointers must still exist in the node structure but point to a null value or a sentinel node. In a typical binary tree, more than half of the nodes are leaves, meaning over half of all child pointers in the entire tree are null and therefore unused. This is highly inefficient.

A **threaded binary tree** solves this problem. [p.1] Instead of leaving the null pointers empty, a threaded binary tree repurposes them to point directly to the node's **in-order predecessor** (the node visited immediately before it during an in-order traversal) or its **in-order successor**. This allows us to traverse the tree without needing extra memory for a stack or recursion. It saves memory by reusing the space that would otherwise be wasted on null pointers.

