{"answer": "PCA and factor analysis look similar but have different goals. PCA is purely a mathematical technique that creates new variables (principal components) by finding the directions of maximum variance in your data, keeping all the original information. Factor analysis, on the other hand, assumes your observed data is generated by a smaller number of hidden, unobserved 'common factors' plus some random noise, and it tries to model this underlying structure [p.1]. The key difference is that PCA tries to explain the total variance in the data, while factor analysis tries to explain only the shared variance, explicitly separating it from unique variance and error [p.1].

Historically, PCA was invented by Karl Pearson in 1901, inspired by the principal axis theorem in mechanics, and was later named and developed by Harold Hotelling in the 1930s [p.1]. Factor analysis developed later, driven by psychologists like Charles Spearman and Louis Thurstone in the early 20th century, who were trying to understand the underlying mental abilities that caused correlations between different psychological test scores [used_general_knowledge].

